[Personal dig: I believe that people who get their thrills saying
"Wrong!!!" probably didn't get enough attention as a child.]

Not true. "Appropriate use of resources" might be a directive stating
that university resources are to be used only for course work. Or it
might state that university resources may not be used for profit or
business purposes.

Harassment, flames, etc. come under the student code of conduct.
Except in very specific criminal cases: libel, harassment, etc., such
activities *do* come under the umbrella of protected speech. At a
private university, some constitutional protections may not apply, but
other ones probably do, as shown in an excellent recent post by Carl
Kadie.

A university that attempts to silence its students with an overbroad
interpretation of "appropriate use" may find itself in a steep
lawsuit. Students silenced thus have been challenging universities and
winning. Any university official that takes such arbitrary action has
probably not yet talked with their lawyer.

A friend of mine was recently asked to attend a meeting with Judicial
Affairs at UCDavis regarding concerns over the "content" of his online
archive. Thanks to a little EFF guidance, he was able to gather a lot
of info, and confidently walked into the meeting with a nice stack of
legal briefs. The issue was resolved amicably and in his favor.
Everyone was educated as to the constitutional and otherwise legal
issues involved, and perhaps less harassment of other students will
result. Perhaps the issue referred to here can be similarly resolved,
with a greater common good resulting.

Sean
